@@ -8776,3 +8776,4 @@ Did NOT modify research-roadmap.yaml or scripts/research_conductor.py. Did NOT p
 - 2026-06-09: SECOND ARC-AGI-3 game solve: solve level-0 of the next-easiest NON-SPATIAL game (lp85-305b61c3 is known-tractable — EWM solves it 100%; su15-1944f8ab / sc25-635fd71a / tn36-ef4dde99 are candidates) via the perception -> induced-model -> goal-predicate loop that produced the r11l first solve. A SECOND solved game is the first evidence the method GENERALIZES beyond r11l (one solve could be game-specific luck; two is a method). Real-env-confirmed; pick the game empirically by L0 action budget + inducibility, do NOT hardcode the win from source. (✅ Complete) — honest_verdict=complete: second_game_solve_lp85-305b61c3_levels1_solvedTrue; results/experiment_3954_second_game_solve.json
 - 2026-06-09: OWED from .365: induce a GOAL-PREDICATE from observed level-up transitions. The M2-v5 finding is that a trustworthy transition-model is necessary but NOT sufficient — you also need to RECOGNIZE the WIN state. Collect level-up transitions (via active/goal-directed play on the non-spatial games), characterize the grid-state property that holds at a win (e.g. all pieces on targets / a matched pattern), and emit a reusable goal_predicate(grid)->bool. Measure: on a held-out set, does the induced predicate correctly flag win-vs-non-win states (precision/recall)? Require >=2 level-up examples before committing a predicate (single-example goal mis-induction is the named M1/M2 risk). (✅ Complete) — honest_verdict=complete: precision_0.40_recall_1.00; results/experiment_3956_goal_predicate_induction.json
 - 2026-06-09: OWED from .365: latent-register augmentation for the 11 HIDDEN-STATE games. The determinism probe (results/arc3_determinism_probe.json) found 11/25 games where the visible grid does NOT determine the dynamics (same frame_hash + action -> different next grid). Augment the state with inferred latent boolean registers (counter / collected-flag / phase) so the augmented state becomes Markov, and measure: does the induced model's consistency energy DROP on the hidden-state games vs the grid-only baseline? This is the named direction-killer (a grid->grid model is under-determined on hidden state); CASSANDRA (arXiv:2601.18620) corroborates programmatic+stochastic world modeling for exactly this regime. (✅ Complete) — honest_verdict=complete: latent_registers_no_drop_energy; results/experiment_3957_hidden_state_latent_registers.json
+- 2026-06-09: CROSS-GAME DSL TRANSFER (self-learning MANDATE, research-program.md Tier-2 constraint memory): build a DreamCoder-style DSL fragment library across the games modeled this milestone, and measure whether reusing the library makes the Nth game's induction CHEAPER than the 1st — fewer codex/proposer calls and/or lower held-out consistency energy at EQUAL active-data budget. This is Carnot's self-learning thesis IN the ARC venue: the agent gets better at NEW games by reusing learned constraint fragments (the persistent GameGraph + DSL library are the Tier-2 memory hardware path). A measured transfer win is the cross-game generalization signal the EWM SOTA also reports (DSL fragment reuse). (✅ Complete) — honest_verdict=complete: dsl_transfer_no_win_no_cost_reduction_observed; results/experiment_3958_cross_game_dsl_transfer.json
